1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e5 Nfd7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. Bd3 a6 8.
Nce2 c5 9. c3 Nc6 10. f4 (10. Nf3 {looks sounder}) 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 Nb4 (
11... Nxd4 {favours Black}) 12. Bb1 Nb6 13. b3 Bd7 14. a3 Nc6 15. Nf3 O-O 16.
Qd3 g6 (16... f5 {may be more solid}) 17. h4 Rac8 18. h5 Be8 19. hxg6 {This
releases the tension, but no constructive plan is immediately obvious. Black
will be playing f6 shortly anyway.} fxg6 20. Ng5 h5 21. g4 {(One of White's
most natural thrusts. Bc2, Qh3, Rh3 can all be considered)} Rxf4 $1 {(Not yet
equalising, but Black's pieces now act harmoniously)} 22. Nxf4 Qxg5 {#} 23.
Rxh5 $2 ({Elaine loses her way in a complex thicket. Barry Wood in The
Illustrated London News recommended} 23. Ne2 {covering c1, but} Nxd4 {is
difficult.}) (23. Qe3 Nxd4 24. Bxg6 {is best according to Fritz)}) 23... Qxf4
24. Ra2 Nxd4 25. Bc2 Nxc2+ 26. Rxc2 Rxc2 27. Qxc2 gxh5 28. gxh5 Qe4+ 0-1

